{"chunk_id":"R2-2502548-001","collection":"ran2_tdoc_chunks","metadata":{"source":["Ericsson"],"tdocNumber":"R2-2502548","title":"Further aspects on  improvements to specification handling"},"point_id":"9218936953323402826"}
In this document we provide our thoughts resulting from the RAN2 trial in Gitlab. If Gitlab were to be adopted as a place for storing the 3GPP specifications we foresee the following work flow. In general, it seems possible to stick to the current work flow we use in 3GPP. Before the meeting: A delegate wanting to propose a change to the specification creates a branch in the Gitlab repository. This branch will be a branch from the “main” branch. The delegate makes the proposed changes, and when its ready implementing its changes it creates a so-called “merge request” and it adds a description of the change. All this happens before the RAN2 submission deadline. Review week: During the “review week” other delegates can review this and all other CRs. If wanted, it would be possible to extract Word-representations of the CRs in Gitlab. But we assume that the true CR is what is found in Gitlab and such Word-representations would just be in case someone prefers to review the CRs in the “old” way. During the online discussion: The chair opens the CR and lets the delegates on the floor to discuss. One approach is that the chair opens the CR in the web browser, another approach is that a Word-representation of the CR is opened. High level agreements are made regarding the CR, e.g. CR may immediately be declared as “not pursued”, or immediately declared as “Agreed”. But often, revising the CR is needed. Today discussions to revise CRs are handled both face-to-face and using “Word-bubbles”. Gitlab does however support this kind of “discussions” and updating of CRs in a much more convenient way compared to the current RAN2 ways of working with the downloading/uploading/adding of Word-bubbles/addressing collisions between different versions/etc. In comparison to Word, it is in fact so convenient to revise CRs in Gitlab that it is questionable if doing this in Word is a feasible way forward. One could however consider that when the revised CR has become stable in the offline discussions, a new Word-representation of the change is created if preferred. But the actual CR should always be what is in Gitlab. It should be noted that generating Word-representation of a CR in Gitlab is something that is already done in the OneM2M standardization group. At the end of the offline discussion, a new revision of the CR is created which can be agreed (or potentially “not pursued”). At plenary: All CRs which were agreed in the WG will at RAN plenary be either approved or rejected (most often they are approved). CR implementation: After the plenary, all CRs which were approved will undergo CR implementation. This means that they are merged into the main branch. Based on the above, we don’t see a need for major deviations form the current ways of working in 3GPP with the introduction of Gitlab. No major change to the current ways of working with CRs is needed if Gitlab is used.

